# EXPECT: inside conditional control flow
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is a limit that two review rounds proved cannot be
# guessed. A module's dict is a SNAPSHOT OF WHAT RAN; the AOT builds it
# statically, and a static walk cannot know which branch executed. The same
# walker was wrong from both sides:
#
#   collecting only the immediate statement list DROPPED a binding made in a
#   TAKEN `if` — `keys of M` lost it and `M.b` read null;
#
#   descending into every body INVENTED one from a NOT-TAKEN `else` — `M.z`
#   read 0, a default-initialised global, so a plausible NUMBER rather than a
#   null. It also picked up `for`-body names the VM does not have at all,
#   because a `for` body gets its own env.
#
# Both were silent, rc 0, zero-byte build log. So the case is refused instead.
# Only the immediate top level and `unobserved:` are unconditionally executed.
#
# Measured cost of refusing: 3 of the 77 stdlib modules (pkg, test_runner,
# ui_draw). This fixture is the first one written against a limit rather than
# a bug, and it exists so the limit cannot silently become a guess again.

import _q1_mod
print of (keys of _q1_mod)
print of _q1_mod.a
